;; =============================================================================
;; Spec: Ariadne step 2e — revocation for cause, and breach history as
;; a routing input
;; =============================================================================
;;
;; Normative refs: docs/architecture/rfc-ariadne-adoption.md (adoption
;; step 2 — "revocation-for-cause"; T3 contradiction 9); Ariadne §13.5
;; (revocation for cause; breach history feeds routing eligibility);
;; explainer #563 "Mary's hall pass" — grants are conditional, and the
;; crossing-off remembers.

{:spec/title "Ariadne step 2e: revocation for cause - a breach ends the grant and is remembered"

 :spec/description
 "Close the loop: a constraint breach revokes the grant that was
  breached, the revocation is durable, and the breach becomes an input
  to future granting rather than a log line nobody reads.

  WHY. Today a budget breach has no consequence beyond the individual
  check that caught it. The runaway is stopped (maybe, on the channels
  that are wired) but nothing about the actor changes — the next run
  gets the same authority. Ariadne's rule is that authority is
  conditional: breach it and the pass is crossed off, and the
  crossing-off is remembered.

  GROUP 1: Breach -> revocation
  When 2b's decide() or 2c's commit re-check finds a constraint
  exceeded, the grant is revoked with :grant/revocation-reason naming
  the axis (:breach/cost-exceeded, :breach/expired,
  :breach/scope-violation). Revocation is transitive over lineage
  (2a's `active?` already walks ancestors) — revoking a parent ends
  every grant cut from it, which is what makes a delegated inner
  orchestrator containable.

  GROUP 2: Un-gateable conditions are AUDITED, not pretended
  Ariadne §13.5's honest half: gates prevent what they can, logbooks
  catch the rest. Some conditions cannot be checked before the fact
  (a cost that only materializes mid-stream, an effect whose true
  outcome arrives later). Those are reconciled by 2c and, when found
  breached after the fact, produce a revocation + an audit record
  rather than a pretence that the gate caught them. A governance
  product that claims to prevent what it can only detect is lying;
  say which is which.

  GROUP 3: Breach history
  A durable, append-only record keyed by principal: what was breached,
  when, on which grant, and whether it was prevented or detected. This
  is the object step 4's routing eligibility consumes. Building the
  RECORD here and the CONSUMPTION later is deliberate — the history
  needs to exist before anything can be scored against it.

  GROUP 4: Eligibility hook (narrow)
  Expose a single predicate — 'may this principal be granted this
  effect class at this ceiling' — that consults breach history. Wire
  it into grant ISSUANCE only. Model-selection routing (the
  `:policy-first routing` line in the RFC's mapping table) is step-3/4
  work and is explicitly NOT wired here; this spec provides the input
  it will eventually call, nothing more.

  SCOPE HONESTY. There is exactly one circuit breaker in the tree
  (`connector-retry/circuit_breaker.clj` — states :closed/:open/
  :half-open, trips at 5 failures / 60s reset) and it has NO
  production callers: grepping allow-request?/record-failure outside
  the component finds only deps.edn entries. It is an HTTP-connector
  breaker, not an authority breaker. This spec does NOT resurrect or
  extend it — revocation is a grant-lifecycle concern, and reusing an
  unconsumed HTTP breaker for it would be the wrong seam. If the
  connector breaker should live or die, that is its own decision."

 :spec/intent {:type :feature
               :scope ["components/execution-grant/"
                       "components/effect-transaction/"]}

 :spec/constraints
 ["Revocation is transitive over lineage - revoking a parent ends descendants"
  "Prevented breaches and detected-after-the-fact breaches are DISTINGUISHABLE in the record"
  "Breach history is append-only - a breach is never edited or deleted"
  "The eligibility predicate is wired into grant issuance ONLY, not into model routing"
  "Do not extend or revive components/connector-retry's circuit breaker for this"
  "Stratified design, max 3 layers per file"]

 :spec/acceptance-criteria
 ["A cost-constraint breach at commit revokes the authorizing grant with :breach/cost-exceeded"
  "Revoking a parent grant makes every descendant grant fail active?"
  "A breach detected during reconciliation (not prevented at decide) is recorded as detected, and a test asserts the two are distinguishable"
  "Breach history survives process restart"
  "Breach history is append-only - a test proves an existing breach record cannot be mutated"
  "The eligibility predicate refuses a ceiling above what the principal's breach history permits"
  "Issuing a grant consults the eligibility predicate; a test proves a breaching principal cannot immediately re-obtain the same ceiling"
  "full bb test green"]

 :spec/priority
 {:tier :high
  :axes #{:correctness :governance-credibility :policy-enforcement}
  :theme :ariadne-grants
  :rationale "Without revocation a grant ceiling is advisory; this is what makes the bound structural rather than a check that can be re-passed indefinitely."
  :blocks []
  :blocked-by ["ariadne-effect-transaction.spec.edn"]}

 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
